Z||000000||Ross||04-18-99||02:35 PM||||Hi guys!!,Imnew to this site,and it looks interesting!!I have an 87SE(they came with the older GT body pkg)thats been in my possesion for ten years now.Shes got 185000 miles on her and has never left me walking!!The IronDuke still runs well,but its time for some new life.The High Output Quad4 is very interesting,and there seems to be several companies building kits for this swap,does any one know who builds a superior kit? Will the stock Isuzu 5sbd handle the extra ponies? 
Z||000001||theogre||04-18-99||03:32 PM||||from every place i've talkted too..... it's not the tranny so much as the clutch that drives everyone nuts with a power boost. Most trannys, even in inline setups, will easilly take a 20-50HP power increase. Clutches on 4 cyls are a whole nuther matter.
Z||000002||PaUL||04-19-99||02:30 PM||||hey, what Ogre says is correct, but Im using an Isuzu 5 spd with a 2.8 v6. The clutch disc and diaphragm from any GM car or truck with the 2.8 V6 will work with the Isuzu trans but youll need the original throughout bearing for the Isuzu. Im using a clutch disc and diaphragm for the getrag 5 spd with a throughout bearing for the Izusu. The clutch has a larger diameter but the splines on the disc are the same. 
Z||000003||PaUL||04-19-99||03:59 PM||||spelling correction: throwout bearing not throughout bearing
Z||000004||theogre||04-19-99||07:58 PM||||check some of the "performance" shops. there should be a counter weighted presure plate to fit these things with at least some of the supported engines. (the direct boltups are listed in another thread.)<p>A counter weighted clutch can tighten up as it spins faster. a standard diaphram can't do this, and often ends up slipping under the loads a strong motor can turn out. You need the clutch to slip slightly as it engages, but if it continues to slip as RPM's rise it won't last long.
